      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im studying for my materials class and im a bit confused. why do we actually need to put steel rebar inside concrete for foundations? i get that concrete is strong, but whats the deal with the relationship between its compressive strength and the tensile strength of the steel? like why do they need each other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Great question! Honestly, concrete is a bit like a goblin with a bad back—it can carry a huge load when you're pressing down on it, but the moment you try to pull it apart, it cracks and crumbles. That's because concrete has excellent compressive strength but very poor tensile strength. Steel rebar, on the other hand, is the wiry, flexible goblin that loves being stretched—it has fantastic tensile strength. So when a foundation bends or shifts, the concrete handles the squeezing while the steel handles the pulling, and together they stop the whole thing from snapping like a dry twig.",
      "source": "target",
