    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Right, exactly! And the real magic is that they work together because they have similar thermal expansion rates—so when temperatures change, they expand and contract at nearly the same pace, avoiding internal stress. That's a crucial detail for foundations, where temperature swings are constant. \n\nIt's a bit like a goblin and a troll teaming up: the goblin is great at squeezing into tight cracks, and the troll is great at holding the cave roof up. Alone, each has a fatal flaw, but together they're unstoppable. \n\nAlso, the steel is usually ribbed or deformed, not smooth, so it physically locks into the concrete—like a goblin's claws gripping a rock. That bond is what transfers the tensile load from the concrete to the steel when the foundation starts to bend. \n\nSo, in short: concrete says \"I'll take the squishing,\" steel says \"I'll take the stretching,\" and the ribs say \"we're in this together, no slipping.\"",
      "source": "target",
