    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i get the thermal expansion part. but what happens if the rebar starts to rust? does that ruin the bond you mentioned with the ribs?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You've hit on one of the classic goblin traps of construction! Yes, rust is the sworn enemy of that ribbed bond. When rebar corrodes, it expands—iron oxide takes up more volume than the original steel—and that expansion acts like a goblin inflating inside a tight tunnel. It pushes outward on the concrete, creating internal tensile stresses that the concrete can't handle, leading to cracking, spalling, and eventually the bond between the ribs and the concrete gets destroyed. \n\nThe rust also acts like a greasy layer, so the concrete can't grip the steel's ribs anymore—the goblin's claws just slide off the slippery, flaky surface. That's why we use protective measures like epoxy coating, galvanizing, or keeping the concrete dense and alkaline (high pH) to passivate the steel and keep it from rusting in the first place. \n\nSo, if rust sets in, you don't just lose the steel's strength—you lose the whole teamwork dynamic, and the foundation starts acting like two goblins arguing over a cave: no grip, no trust, and eventually everything crumbles.",
